{#
  module/Books/view/books/texts/index.phtml plus its own search-bar.phtml, ported.

  Two states, and the second is most of the page. With results: the search box and one
  `<p>` per text — a bold `format_entity` link, then an excerpt with the search term
  wrapped in `<mark>`. With none: the search box and a markdown block explaining what the
  corpus is and who may read it.

  The two are `{% if objects is defined %}` in the .phtml rather than a check on their
  contents, which matters: a search that matches nothing sets `$objects = []`, and `[]`
  *is* set, so the empty state renders the box, the "No results found." message from the
  controller, and **no explanatory text**. Reproduced with `is not null`, which is the
  same test — the action initialises `$objects = null` and only assigns on a valid query.

  The prose below is copied from the .phtml's heredoc, wording and line breaks included —
  it renders through the markdown helper, so a reflow would change the rendered
  paragraphs. Two of its lines end in a space in the original and **only one of them
  survives**: CommonMark strips a single trailing space inside a paragraph but keeps one
  at the end of it, so laminas emits `...Once we have that\ninformation...` and
  `...a little nicer format. </p>`. Measured, not assumed. The surviving space is written
  literally on the "format." line and is the kind of thing an editor set to trim trailing
  whitespace will silently eat, so Batch6SymfonySmokeTest asserts the exact closing bytes.
#}
{% extends 'layout.html.twig' %}

{% block content %}
{% include 'books/_texts-search-bar.html.twig' with {'form': form, 'center_on_page': false} only %}
{% if objects is not null %}
    {% import 'schoenstatt/_entity-format.html.twig' as format %}
    {% for object in objects %}
<p><strong>{{ format.entity('text', object) }}</strong>&nbsp;{% if search is not null %}{{
    excerpts[object.textId]|default('')|raw }}{% endif %}</p>
    {% endfor %}
{% else %}
{{ markdown(
"## To whomever may stumble across this page...

This is a work in progress to make Fr. Kentenich available to anyone
who is interested.

So far we have only imported the texts, but not yet parsed the metadata
describing the day on which each conference took place and the publication
from which it was extracted. Once we have that
information, we can make present this information in a little nicer format. 

In any case, the texts are already searchable.

In the near future we hope to apply some artificial intelligence to abstract
brief summaries of each text, translate them to various language, and extract
keywords, concepts and person/place references from each.

The page should be accessible to any father who makes an account on this site
using the same email address as is recorded on schoenstatt-fathers.link. This
provides some simple, but precise access control until we decide as a movement
to open up Fr. Kentenich's writings to everyone."
) }}
{% endif %}
{% endblock %}
